room      lemma_2
kind      geometry
import    Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Book I, Section I, Lemma II; Plate 1, Fig. 6.
caption   If in any figure AacE there be inscribed parallelograms on equal bases and circumscribed parallelograms completed: as the bases are diminished without limit, the inscribed, circumscribed, and curvilinear figures have the ultimate ratio of equality.
final     3

station 1
  ceiling   eq1 :: \text{Area}(AacE)=\int_{A}^{E} f\,dx
  gloss   The curvilinear figure is bounded below by AE, at left by Aa, and above by the curve acE, with the base cut into equal parts.
  color   curve #1f6feb
  color   base #b8860b
  panel
    point   A @(0,0)  label=$A$ at=SW
    point   E @(9,0)  label=$E$ at=SE
    point   a @(0,4)  label=$a$ at=NW
    point   B @(3,0)  label=$B$ at=S
    point   C @(6,0)  label=$C$ at=S
    point   D @(9,0)
    point   p1 @(1.5,3.6)
    point   p2 @(4.5,2.6)
    point   p3 @(7.5,1.3)
    segment ae A E  color=base  label=$AE$ at=S
    segment aa A a
    polyline curve a p1 p2 p3 E  color=curve  heart  label=$acE$ at=NE
    segment ab A B  color=base
    segment bc B C  color=base
    segment cd C D  color=base
  text
    Newton considers a plane figure $AacE$ bounded by the straight side {base|Aa},
    the baseline {base|AE}, and the curve {curve|acE}.
    The baseline is divided into the equal bases $AB$, $BC$, $CD$, so that {base|the base}
    is partitioned into segments of one common breadth.
    The whole question concerns the area enclosed beneath {curve|the curve} —
    for it is {curve|this curve} that is the heart of the figure and of the Lemma.

station 2
  ceiling   eq2 :: I_n=\sum_{k} f(x_k)\,\Delta x \le \text{Area}(AacE)
  gloss   The inscribed rectangles rise only to the lower corner of the curve on each base, lying wholly beneath it.
  color   insc #cf3b2f
  color   curve #1f6feb
  panel
    point   A @(0,0)  label=$A$ at=SW
    point   E @(9,0)  label=$E$ at=SE
    point   a @(0,4)  label=$a$ at=NW
    point   B @(3,0)  label=$B$ at=S
    point   C @(6,0)  label=$C$ at=S
    point   D @(9,0)  label=$D$ at=SE
    point   b @(3,2.6)  label=$b$ at=NE
    point   c @(6,1.3)  label=$c$ at=NE
    segment ae2 A E
    polyline curve a b c E  color=curve  label=$acE$ at=NE
    polygon pa A B b a  color=insc  heart
    polygon pb B C c b  color=insc  heart
    polygon pc C D E c  color=insc  heart
  text
    Within the figure Newton inscribes {insc|the parallelograms $Ab$, $Bc$, $Cd$},
    each raised on one of the equal bases and reaching upward only to the lower vertex
    of {curve|the curve} on that base.
    Because {insc|each inscribed rectangle} stops short of {curve|the curve},
    their united area — the step-figure $AKbLcMdD$ — lies {insc|entirely below} it.
    Thus {insc|the inscribed figure} is a deficient approximation, always less than
    the true curvilinear area.

station 3
  ceiling   eq3 :: C_n-I_n=AB\cdot Aa \;\longrightarrow\; 0
  gloss   The circumscribed rectangles overtop the curve; their total excess collapses into the single vanishing rectangle ABla.
  color   circ #2ea043
  color   excess #cf3b2f
  color   curve #1f6feb
  panel
    point   A @(0,0)  label=$A$ at=SW
    point   E @(9,0)  label=$E$ at=SE
    point   a @(0,4)  label=$a$ at=NW
    point   l @(0,3.6)  label=$l$ at=W
    point   B @(3,0)  label=$B$ at=S
    point   C @(6,0)  label=$C$ at=S
    point   D @(9,0)
    point   b @(3,3.6)  label=$b$ at=NE
    point   c @(6,2.6)  label=$c$ at=NE
    point   e_top @(9,1.3)
    point   c_bot @(6,1.3)
    segment ae3 A E
    polyline curve a b c E  color=curve  label=$acE$ at=NE
    polygon pa A B b a  color=circ  heart
    polygon pb B C c b  color=circ  heart
    polygon pc C D e_top c_bot  color=circ  heart
    polygon pex A B b l  color=excess  label=$ABla$ at=N
  text
    Finally Newton circumscribes {circ|the parallelograms $aKbl$, $bLcm$, $cMdn$},
    each rising above {curve|the curve} to its upper vertex.
    The difference between {circ|the circumscribed figure} and the inscribed figure
    is the sum of the little tops $Kl$, $Lm$, $Mn$, $Do$; since all bases are equal,
    this sum is {excess|the single rectangle $ABla$}, of breadth $AB$ and height $Aa$.
    But as $AB$ is diminished $in\ infinitum$, {excess|this rectangle} becomes less
    than any given space, so by Lemma~I the inscribed and {circ|circumscribed} figures
    become ultimately equal, and $much\ more$ is {curve|the intermediate curvilinear figure}
    equal to either.
    $Q.E.D.$
